><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidetic was an Anatolian language spoken at Side on the Pamphylian coast of southern Turkey, related to Luwian and Lycian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidi-ahmed-ou-moussa"><span class="enc-name">Sidi Ahmed ou Moussa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidi Ahmed ou Moussa was a Sufi saint of the Sous region of southern Morocco who taught in the Tazeroualt and died there in 1563.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidi-bel-abbes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidi-bel-abbes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidi Bel Abbès</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidi Bel Abbès is a city in north-western Algeria, south of Oran, that grew from a French military post established in the 1840s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidi-fredj"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidi-fredj.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidi Fredj</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidi Fredj, known to the French as Sidi Ferruch, is a small peninsula on the Mediterranean coast about 25 km west of Algiers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidi-saiyyed-mosque"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidi-saiyyed-mosque.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidi Saiyyed Mosque</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sidi Saiyyed Mosque is a mosque in Ahmedabad, Gujarat, built in 1572–1573 in the last years of the Gujarat Sultanate by Sidi Saiyyed, a noble of Abyssinian origin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidney-hill"><span class="enc-name">Sidney Hill</span><span class="enc-type">Person</