"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidon-sea-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidon-sea-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidon Sea Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sidon Sea Castle is a crusader fortress built in 1228 on a small island off the harbour of Sidon in Lebanon, linked to the shore by a causeway.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidonio-pais"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidonio-pais.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidónio Pais</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidónio Pais was a Portuguese army officer and politician who seized power in a coup in December 1917 and ruled the First Portuguese Republic as an authoritarian…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidonius-apollinaris"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidonius-apollinaris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidonius Apollinaris</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidonius Apollinaris was a Gallo-Roman aristocrat, poet and bishop of Clermont from about 470.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidqa"><span class="enc-name">Sidqa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidqa was king of the Philistine city of Ashkelon in the late 8th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidray-ibn-wazir"><span class="enc-name">Sidray ibn Wazir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidray ibn Wazir was a commander of the Banu Wazir family in the west of al-Andalus who, in the revolt against the Almoravids in 1144, took Beja and Évora and ruled…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siebengebirge"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siebengebirge.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siebengebirge</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siebengebirge ('Seven Mountains') is a range of volcanic hills on the east bank of the Rhine south-east of Bonn, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sieg-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sieg-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sieg (river)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sieg is a river in western Germany, about 155 km long, rising in the Rothaar Mountains and flowing west through the Siegerland to join the Rhine near Bonn.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-capability"><span class="enc-name">Siege capability</span><span class="enc-type">Historical factor</span><span class="enc-snip">Siege capability refers to the recurring pattern in which an empire's ability to capture fortified cities determined how far it could expand.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-engine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege engine</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A siege engine is a device designed to break or bypass fortifications, such as a battering ram, siege tower, catapult or trebuchet.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-acre-1189-1191"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Acre (1189–1191)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Acre (1189–1191) was a long siege of the port city of Acre during the Third Crusade.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-akhulgo"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Akhulgo</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1839</span><span class="enc-snip">The siege of Akhulgo was a Russian siege of Imam Shamil's mountain fortress in Dagestan in the summer of 1839.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-alamut"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-alamut.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Alamut</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1256</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Alamut was the capture of the mountain fortress of the Nizari Ismailis in northern Iran by Mongol forces under Hulagu in 1256.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-amida-359"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Amida (359)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 359 CE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Amida in 359 CE was the capture of the Roman city of Amida, modern Diyarbakir, by the Sasanian king Shapur II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-antioch-256"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Antioch (256)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 256 CE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Antioch in 256 CE was the capture of the great Roman city of Antioch by the Sasanian king Shapur I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-baghdad-1258"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-baghdad-1258.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Baghdad (1258)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1258</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Baghdad in 1258 was the capture and sack of the Abbasid capital by the Mongol army of Hulagu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-basra-1775-1776"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Basra (1775–1776)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1775–1776</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Basra (1775–1776) was the capture of the Ottoman-held port city of Basra by the forces of the Zand Empire under Karim Khan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-belgrade-1521"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Belgrade (1521)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1521</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Belgrade in 1521 was the capture of the Hungarian fortress city by the Ottoman sultan Suleiman the Magnificent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-bursa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-bursa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Bursa</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1326</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Bursa was the long Ottoman blockade of the Byzantine city of Bursa in north-western Anatolia, which surrendered in 1326 to Orhan, son of Osman I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-caizhou"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Caizhou</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1233–1234</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Caizhou in 1233 and 1234 was the final campaign against the Jurchen Jin dynasty in China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-calvi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-calvi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Calvi</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1794</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Calvi was fought from June to August 1794, when British forces and Corsican allies besieged the French garrison of Calvi in north-western Corsica.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-carthage-149-146-bce"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Carthage (149–146 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 146 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Carthage was the main engagement of the Third Punic War, in which Roman forces besieged the city of Carthage.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-o